  ],
  "definition": "A training-free, inference-time steering method that treats overthinking in a reasoning model as movement along a low-dimensional manifold of its residual-stream activations, recovered via PCA. It extracts an 'overthinking direction' by difference-in-means between overthinking and non-overthinking activations, projects that direction onto the PCA manifold, and ablates the projected component at inference time -- a step motivated by the finding that ablating the raw, unprojected difference-in-means direction only reduces overthinking up to a point, after which its effect plateaus and then reverses as intervention strength increases, corrupting other model abilities. The source paper reports this projection removes that accuracy-degrading interference noise and cuts output tokens by up to 71% while maintaining or improving accuracy. The 'Don't Overthink It' survey classifies it under 'Representation Engineering', alongside other steering-vector methods (SEAL, Pre-allocated Direction Vectors, Thinking Progress Vector).",
  "evidence": [
